Closure Declared Inside Another Closure's Body
==================================================

Reference §"Closures and Function Pointers". When the BODY of one
closure declares another (locally-defined) closure, the inner's
captures resolve against the outer's bindings and the outer's own
capture set MUST NOT spuriously include the inner's locally-bound
name.

This was a real compiler bug fixed at sysl@71fd9519 (2026-04-26).
See `feedback_sysl_closure_in_closure.md`. The bug surfaced when the
inner closure was bound with a bare-assignment (`inner = ...`)
instead of an explicit `val inner = ...`: AssignStmtAST's lowering
treated the bare-binding as a write to an outer-scope name (it
isn't), so the capture-detection pass added `inner` to the outer
closure's capture list. The outer then tried to look up `inner` in
its DEFINING environment — where `inner` was supposed to be bound
by a body statement that hadn't yet executed — and crashed with
"undefined variable: inner".

Sysl's closure-literal grammar accepts only single-expression
bodies (`(x) -> expr`); multi-stmt bodies have to go through a
helper. The tests below isolate the closure-in-closure shape via
factory helpers whose body returns a freshly-constructed closure
that captures the outer's parameter (and may itself invoke another
locally-constructed closure).

File-local prefix `Cic` (Closure-In-Closure).

    module sysl.tests.lang_features


Local closure that captures the outer's param
-----------------------------------------------

### `make_adder(n)` returns a closure that adds n

The simplest closure-of-fn shape: outer is a regular fn (not a
closure), inner is a closure that captures the outer's param.

    cic_make_adder(n: int) -> (int) -> int = (x: int) -> x + n

    #test
    test_cic_make_adder() -> unit
        val add10 = cic_make_adder(10)
        assert(add10(5) == 15, "10+5")
        assert(add10(0) == 10, "10+0")


Inner declared with bare-binding inside a multi-stmt outer
-------------------------------------------------------------

### Multi-stmt outer body locally binds an inner closure

The outer body uses `val inner = (...) -> ...` (the form covered by
the 2026-04-26 fix). The outer fn then invokes the inner closure
and returns the result.

    cic_invoke_inner(base: int, x: int) -> int
        val inner = (y: int) -> y + base
        return inner(x) + 1

    #test
    test_cic_inner_basic() -> unit
        assert(cic_invoke_inner(10, 5) == 16, "(5+10) + 1")
        assert(cic_invoke_inner(10, 0) == 11, "(0+10) + 1")
        assert(cic_invoke_inner(0, 0) == 1, "0+0+1")


Two inner closures coexist in one outer body
----------------------------------------------

### Both `add` and `mul` declared inside; both reference outer's `base`

Pins that two locally-declared closures in one fn body each see
the outer's captures and don't shadow each other.

    cic_two_inners(base: int, x: int) -> int
        val add = (n: int) -> n + base
        val mul = (n: int) -> n * base
        return add(x) + mul(x)

    #test
    test_cic_two_inners() -> unit
        // f(4, 5) -> (5+4) + (5*4) = 9 + 20 = 29
        assert(cic_two_inners(4, 5) == 29, "9+20 = 29")
        // base=0: 0 + 0 = 0
        assert(cic_two_inners(0, 7) == 7, "7+0 = 7")


Inner closure invoked twice within outer body
-----------------------------------------------

### Same closure value called multiple times, captures stable

Re-invocation must resolve against the SAME captures — no
re-binding between calls.

    cic_inner_twice(scale: int, x: int) -> int
        val add_scale = (n: int) -> n + scale
        return add_scale(add_scale(x))

    #test
    test_cic_inner_twice() -> unit
        // x=5, scale=3 → add_scale(add_scale(5)) = add_scale(8) = 11
        assert(cic_inner_twice(3, 5) == 11, "5+3+3 = 11")
        assert(cic_inner_twice(10, 0) == 20, "0+10+10 = 20")


Closure-returning fn called from inside another fn's body
-----------------------------------------------------------

### Factory used inside a wrapper fn

Combines two mechanisms: an inner fn invokes a factory to get a
closure, then calls it with its own argument. Pins that values
returned by sub-calls remain valid through the wrapper's body.

    cic_wrap(base: int, x: int) -> int
        val f = cic_make_adder(base)
        return f(x)

    #test
    test_cic_wrap() -> unit
        assert(cic_wrap(7, 3) == 10, "7+3")
        assert(cic_wrap(100, 0) == 100, "100+0")


Two distinct closures from same factory don't alias
-----------------------------------------------------

### Two factory calls produce closures with disjoint captures

Pins that the factory's `n` parameter is captured separately into
each closure's env — modifying one shouldn't affect the other.

    #test
    test_cic_two_distinct() -> unit
        val add3 = cic_make_adder(3)
        val add9 = cic_make_adder(9)
        assert(add3(10) == 13, "add3")
        assert(add9(10) == 19, "add9")
        // Interleave calls
        assert(add3(1) + add9(1) == 14, "4 + 10 = 14")
        assert(add3(0) == 3, "add3 still 3")
        assert(add9(0) == 9, "add9 still 9")
